   "timeFrame": "At Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Participants Achieving 15% Weight Loss or More (Yes/No)",
   "description": "Participants achieving 15% weight loss or more (Yes/No) at week 52 is presented. In the reported data, 'Yes' infers number of participants who have achieved 15% weight loss or more whereas 'No' infers number of participants who have not achieved 15% weight loss or more. The outcome data was evaluated based on the in-trial observation period. The in-trial period was defined as the time interval from date of randomisation to date of last contact with trial site (or date of withdrawal of informed consent or death).",
   "timeFrame": "At Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Participants Achieving 20% Weight Loss or More (Yes/No)",
   "description": "Participants achieving 20% weight loss or more (Yes/No) at week 52 is presented. In the reported data, 'Yes' infers number of participants who have achieved 20% weight loss or more whereas 'No' infers number of participants who have not achieved 20% weight loss or more. The outcome data was evaluated based on the in-trial observation period. The in-trial period was defined as the time interval from date of randomisation to date of last contact with trial site (or date of withdrawal of informed consent or death).",
   "timeFrame": "At Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Participants Improving 5 Points or More in KCCQ Clinical Summary Score (Yes/No)",
   "description": "Participants improving 5 points or more in KCCQ clinical summary score (Yes/No) at week 52 is presented. The KCCQ is a standardised 23-item, self-administered instrument that quantifies heart failure symptoms (frequency, severity, and recent change), physical limitation, quality of life (QOL), and social limitation. Scores were transformed to a range of 0 to 100 in which higher scores reflected better health status. KCCQ-CSS included the symptom and physical limitation domains of the KCCQ. In the reported data, 'Yes' infers number of participants who have improved 5 points or more in score whereas 'No' infers number of participants who have not improved 5 points or more in score. The outcome data was evaluated based on the in-trial observation period. The in-trial period was defined as the time interval from date of randomisation to date of last contact with trial site (or date of withdrawal of informed consent or death).",
   "timeFrame": "At Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Participants Improving 10 Points or More in KCCQ Clinical Summary Score (Yes/No)",
   "description": "Participants improving 10 points or more in KCCQ clinical summary score (Yes/No) at week 52 is presented. The KCCQ is a standardised 23-item, self-administered instrument that quantifies heart failure symptoms (frequency, severity, and recent change), physical limitation, quality of life (QOL), and social limitation. Scores were transformed to a range of 0 to 100 in which higher scores reflected better health status. KCCQ-CSS included the symptom and physical limitation domains of the KCCQ. In reported data, 'Yes' infers number of participants who have improved 10 points or more in score whereas 'No' infers number of participants who have not improved 10 points or more in score. The outcome data was evaluated based on the in-trial observation period. The in-trial period was defined as the time interval from date of randomisation to date of last contact with trial site (or date of withdrawal of informed consent or death).",
   "timeFrame": "At Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Change in KCCQ Overall Summary Score",
   "description": "Change in KCCQ overall summary score from baseline (week 0) to end of treatment (week 52) is presented. The KCCQ is a standardised 23-item, self-administered instrument that quantifies heart failure symptoms (frequency, severity, and recent change), physical limitation, QOL, and social limitation. Scores are transformed to a range of 0 to 100 in which higher scores reflect better health status. KCCQ- overall summary score (OSS) included the symptom, physical limitation, quality of life, and social limitation domains. The outcome data was evaluated based on the in-trial observation period. The in-trial period was defined as the time interval from date of randomisation to date of last contact with trial site (or date of withdrawal of informed consent or death).",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline (week 0), end of treatment (week 52)"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Participants Achieving Threshold for Clinically Meaningful Within-participant Change in KCCQ-CSS (PGI-S)",
   "description": "Participants achieving threshold for clinically meaningful within-participant change (based on participant perspective) in KCCQ-CSS (Patient Global Impression of Status \\[PGI-S\\]) at week 52 is presented. KCCQ is a standardised 23-item instrument that quantifies heart failure symptoms (frequency, severity, and recent change), physical limitation, QOL, and social limitation. Scores are transformed to a range of 0 to 100 where higher scores reflect better health status. KCCQ-CSS includes the symptom and physical limitation domains of KCCQ. PGI-S for KCCQ was used to rate participants' symptoms of heart failure in last two weeks using a 4-category ordinal scale (no symptoms, mild, moderate, severe). The threshold of 16.3 points is based on PGI-S for KCCQ-CSS. Outcome data was evaluated based on in-trial period. In-trial period was defined as time interval from date of randomisation to date of last contact with trial site (or date of withdrawal of informed consent or death).",
   "timeFrame": "At Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Participants Achieving Threshold for Clinically Meaningful Within-participant Change in Six Minute Walking Distance (6MWD) (PGI-S)",
   "description": "Participants achieving threshold for clinically meaningful within-participant change in 6MWD (PGI-S) at week 52 is presented. The six minute walk test (6MWT) is a direct and timed measure of walk distance. The goal is for the subject to walk as far as possible in six minutes without running. The PGI-S for 6MWT was used to rate any difficulty that participants were experiencing in walking quickly using a 5-category ordinal scale (not at all difficult, a little difficult, moderately difficult, very difficult, or unable to walk quickly). The threshold of 16.2 meters is based on PGI-S for 6MWT. The outcome data was evaluated based on the in-trial observation period. The in-trial period was defined as the time interval from date of randomisation to date of last contact with trial site (or date of withdrawal of informed consent or death).",
   "timeFrame": "At Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Change in Waist Circumference",
