    "Placebo"
   ]
  }
 ],
 "primaryOutcomes": [
  {
   "measure": "Change From Baseline in Clinician Administered PTSD Scale for DSM-5 (Visit 6 - Baseline)",
   "description": "The CAPS is the gold standard in PTSD assessment. The CAPS-5 is a 30-item structured interview that can be used to make a diagnosis of PTSD and assess PTSD symptoms. It assesses the intensity and frequency of PTSD symptoms. Scores range from 0-80; higher score indicates greater severity.",
   "timeFrame": "Study drug onset to treatment week 8"
  }
 ],
 "secondaryOutcomes": [
  {
   "measure": "Change From Baseline in Brief Pain Inventory, Short Form (Visit 6 - Baseline): Average Pain",
   "description": "The Brief Pain Inventory, Short Form (BPI-SF) is a self-reported scale that measures the severity of pain and the interference of pain on function. The scores range from 0 (no pain) to 10 (pain as severe as you can imagine). There are 4 questions assessing worst pain in past 24 hrs, least pain in past 24 hrs, average pain, and pain right now. The Interference scores range from 0 (does not interfere) to 10 (completely interferes). There are 7 questions assessing the interference of pain in the past 24 hours for general activity, mood, walking ability, normal work, relations with other people, sleep, and enjoyment of life.",
   "timeFrame": "Study drug onset to treatment week 8"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Change From Baseline in Brief Pain Inventory, Short Form (Visit 6 - Baseline): Average Interference Across 7 Types of Activity",
   "description": "The Brief Pain Inventory, Short Form (BPI-SF) is a self-reported scale that measures the severity of pain and the interference of pain on function. The scores range from 0 (no pain) to 10 (pain as severe as you can imagine). There are 4 questions assessing worst pain in past 24 hrs, least pain in past 24 hrs, average pain, and pain right now. The Interference scores range from 0 (does not interfere) to 10 (completely interferes). There are 7 questions assessing the interference of pain in the past 24 hours for general activity, mood, walking ability, normal work, relations with other people, sleep, and enjoyment of life.",
   "timeFrame": "Study drug onset to treatment week 8"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Change From Baseline in Hamilton-Depression Inventory (Visit 6 - Baseline)",
   "description": "The HAM-D measures the severity of depressive symptoms. It is a checklist of 17 items that are ranked on a scale of 0-4 or 0-2. The range for the total score (which is the sum of the scores of all 17 items) is 0-52; a higher score indicates greater severity of symptoms.",
   "timeFrame": "Study drug onset to treatment week 8"
  }
 ],
 "eligibility": {
  "criteria": "Inclusion Criteria:\n\n* At study outset: DSM-5 diagnosis of PTSD with CAPS-5 Total Score \\>=30. Following protocol amendment effective 4/13/2023: criterion modified to require diagnosis of PTSD using the CAPS-5 definition, defined by the following: at least one Criterion B symptom, at least one Criterion C symptom, at least 2 Criterion D symptoms and at least 2 Criterion E symptoms. This thus replaced the firm cutoff of CAPS-5 Total Score \\>=30.\n* Females will be required to use a medically and study approved contraceptive or otherwise not be of child-bearing potential\n\n  * Birth control methods must be non-hormonal\n* No anticipated need to alter psychiatric medications for duration of study involvement\n* Ability to participate fully in the informed consent process\n\nExclusion Criteria:\n\n* History of allergy to pregnenolone\n* Medical disorders that may preclude safe administration of pregnenolone or exacerbate PTSD symptoms\n* Current suicidal or homicidal ideation necessitating clinical intervention or representing an imminent concern\n\n  * Prior suicide attempt history or suicidal ideation that does not require clinical intervention or represent an imminent concern is permitted\n* Serious unstable medical illness, such as:\n\n  * history of cerebrovascular accident\n  * prostate, uterine, or breast cancer\n  * others (at the discretion of the PI and medical oversight team)\n* Medical conditions not well controlled will be excluded, at the discretion of the PI and Medical Team\n* Standard pharmacological interventions for PTSD will not be exclusionary, including, but not limited to:\n\n  * antidepressant medications such as SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics, bupropion, mirtazapine, venlafaxine, and nefazodone\n  * mood stabilizers such as carbamazepine, divalproex, lamotrigine, topiramate\n  * atypical antipsychotics, and other agents including prazosin\n  * However, there may be no changes in psychotropic medications for PTSD 4 weeks prior to study randomization\n* Benzodiazepine use\n* Current diagnosis of bipolar disorder, schizophrenia or other psychotic disorder, or cognitive disorder due to a general medical condition other than mild TBI (assessed at screening)\n* Initiation or change in psychotherapy within 3 months of randomization, i.e. psychotherapy must be stable for 3 months prior to study start\n* Participants on hormonal therapies such as finasteride or hormonal birth control\n* Female participants who are pregnant or breast-feeding\n* As indicated by the DSM-5, moderate or severe Substance Use Disorders (excluding caffeine and tobacco) within 1 month of study entry\n\n  * Mild Alcohol Use Disorder is not exclusionary, at the judgment of the PI and her medical team",
  "healthyVolunteers": false,
  "sex": "ALL",
  "minimumAge": "18 Years",
  "maximumAge": "65 Years"
 },
 "siteCount": 1,
 "countries": [
  "United States"
 ]
}

