  {
   "measure": "Change in SF-36 V2 Physical Component Summary Scale From Baseline to Weeks 24 and 52",
   "description": "The SF-36 is a multi-purpose, short-form health survey with 36 questions. It yields an 8-scale profile of functional health and well-being scores (domains) as well as psychometrically based physical and mental health summary measures. The SF-36 taps 8 health concepts: physical functioning, role limitations due to physical health, bodily pain, general health perceptions, vitality, social functioning, role limitations due to emotional problems, and mental health. The 8 scales are further summarized to 2 distinct higher-ordered clusters: the PCS and the MCS. The range for all 8 domains as well as for the composite t-scores is from 0 to 100 with 100 as best possible health status and 0 as worst health status. Adjusted mean values have been reported. The analysis was performed using ANCOVA model.",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline, Weeks 24 and 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Change in Active Joint Count From Baseline to Weeks 24 and 52",
   "description": "Active joint was defined as a joint that is both swollen and tender. A 28-joint count of swollen and tender joints was performed in this study. Data after the first occurrence of intercurrent event (ICE), including major concomitant therapy violation, treatment failure requiring rescue medication or early discontinuation from the blinded period due to AE, death and lack of efficacy, was imputed using the worst post-baseline value from the following assessments: the last value available prior to the occurrence of the first ICE; all values available after the occurrence of the ICE. Participants with treatment discontinuation without any ICE above were evaluated under the treatment policy strategy. Adjusted mean values have been reported. The analysis was performed using ANCOVA model.",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline, Weeks 24 and 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Percentage of Participants Who Achieved a >=50% Reduction in Active Joint Counts at Each Study Visit, Among Participants With >=4 Active Joints at Baseline",
   "description": "Active joint was defined as a joint that is both swollen and tender. A 28-joint count of swollen and tender joints was performed in this study. Participants who experienced major concomitant therapy violation, treatment failure requiring rescue medication, and discontinuation from the double-blinded period due to death, lack of efficacy or AE were considered non-responders.",
   "timeFrame": "At Weeks 24, 28, 32, 36, 40, 44, 48 and 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Percentage of Participants Who Achieved a >=50% Reduction in Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus Disease Area and Severity (CLASI) Total Activity Score at Each Study Visit",
   "description": "CLASI is a validated instrument for assessing cutaneous lupus erythematosus activity. It reports two global scores: one for current inflammatory activity (Total Activity Score), and one for damage (Total Damage Score). To calculate the total scores, trained assessors evaluate erythema, scale/hypertrophy, dyspigmentation, and scarring/atrophy/panniculitis at 13 anatomic locations plus additional assessments for mucosal lesions and alopecia. CLASI total activity score ranges from 0 to 70, with higher scores indicating increased disease severity. Participants who experienced major concomitant therapy violation, treatment failure requiring rescue medication, and discontinuation from the double-blinded period due to death, lack of efficacy or AE were considered non-responders.",
   "timeFrame": "At Weeks 24, 28, 32, 36, 40, 44, 48 and 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Percentage of Participants Who Achieved Sustained OCS (<=5 mg/Day) From Week 40 Through Week 52",
   "description": "Sustained OCS was defined as all of the following: 1) No treatment with prednisone \\>5 mg/day (or equivalent) from Week 40 through Week 52; 2) No receipt of intravenous, intramuscular, or intra-articular corticosteroids from Week 40 through Week 52. Participants who experienced intercurrent events, including major concomitant therapy violation, treatment failure requiring rescue medication, and discontinuation from the double-blinded period due to death, lack of efficacy, or AE, were considered nonresponders.",
   "timeFrame": "From Week 40 through Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Cumulative Corticosteroid Use Through Week 52",
   "description": "Cumulative oral corticosteroid was represented in prednisone or equivalent only. The mean of the standardized area under the curve (AUC) through Week 52 have been reported. AUC was derived based on the actual dose participants have received. Standardized AUC was derived using AUC divided by days in study multiplied by 365.25 days for each participant.",
   "timeFrame": "Up to Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Annualized Flare Rate Through Week 52",
   "description": "BILAG flare was defined as the occurrence of \\>=1 new BILAG A or \\>=2 new BILAG B manifestations from the previous visit. BILAG Index assesses clinical signs, symptoms, and laboratory parameters related to SLE across 9 organ systems. Each organ system is scored alphabetically: A (severe disease), B (moderate disease), C (mild stable disease), D (inactive but previously active), and E (inactive and never affected). The number of flares over the 52-week blinded treatment period has been reported. The number is calculating rate, which is the number of events (flares) over total participant-years (time period).",
   "timeFrame": "Up to Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Number of Participants With Adverse Events (AEs)",
   "description": "An AE was any untoward medical occurrence in a clinical study participant temporally associated with the use of a study treatment, whether or not considered related to the study treatment. An AE can therefore be any unfavorable and unintended sign (including an abnormal laboratory finding), symptom, or disease (new or exacerbated) temporally associated with the use of study intervention.",
   "timeFrame": "Up to Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Number of Participants With Adverse Events of Special Interests (AESIs), Including Infusion-related Reactions (IRRs), Neutropenia, Infections, and Thrombocytopenia",
   "description": "AESIs for this study were as follows: cases of potential drug-induced liver injury that include an elevated alanine aminotransferase (ALT) or aspartate aminotransferase (AST) in combination with either an elevated bilirubin or clinical jaundice; suspected transmission of an infectious agent by a study treatment (Hy's Law); IRRs (systemic IRRs only); grade \\>=3 infections; any hepatitis B reactivation; progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML); study drug-related neutropenia; study drug-related thrombocytopenia; gastrointestinal (GI) perforations; worsening of preexisting cardiac conditions.",
   "timeFrame": "Up to Week 52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Maximum Serum Concentration (Cmax) of Obinutuzumab",
   "description": "Cmax is defined as the maximum concentration of a drug in the serum. Cmax is reported at steady state. Hence, only one value for the specified assessment periods is reported here.",
   "timeFrame": "From Weeks 0-24 and Weeks 24-52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Trough Concentration (Ctrough) of Obinutuzumab",
   "description": "Ctrough is defined as the concentration immediately prior to the next dose at steady state (end of the dosing interval). Data were collected when the participant attended the next dose administration visits over the two time intervals.",
   "timeFrame": "From Weeks 0-24 and Weeks 24-52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Area Under the Concentration-time Curve (AUC) of Obinutuzumab",
   "description": "AUC is defined as the area under the drug concentration-time curve and represents drug exposure over a specified time interval. Data presented here give exposure over the duration of 0 to 52 weeks.",
   "timeFrame": "Week 0-52"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Number of Participants With Anti-drug Antibodies (ADAs) to Obinutuzumab",
   "description": "Participants who received obinutuzumab were considered to be treatment-emergent ADA-positive if they were ADA-negative or had missing data at baseline but developed an ADA response following obinutuzumab exposure (treatment-induced ADA response), or if they were ADA-positive at baseline and the titer of one or more post-baseline samples was at least 0.60 titer units (t.u.) greater than the titer of the baseline sample (treatment-enhanced ADA response). Participants with a positive post-baseline sample has been reported here.",
   "timeFrame": "Up to Week 52"
  }
 ],
 "eligibility": {
  "criteria": "Inclusion Criteria:\n\n* Diagnosis of SLE according to the 2019 European League Against Rheumatism/American College of Rheumatology (EULAR/ACR) Classification Criteria \\>=12 weeks prior to screening\n* Anti-nuclear antibody (ANA) \\>=1:80, or anti-dsDNA and/or anti-Sm antibodies above the upper limit of normal (ULN), as determined by the central laboratory at screening\n* Low C3 or low C4 or low CH50 complement as determined by the central laboratory at screening\n* High disease activity at screening, based on; BILAG-2004 (Category A disease in \\>=1 organ system and/or Category B disease in \\>=2 organ systems), Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Disease Activity Index 2000 (SLEDAI-2K) (score \\>=8) and Physician's Global Assessment (PGA) (score \\>=1.0 on a 0 to 3 visual analogue scale \\[VAS\\])\n* High disease activity on Day 1, based on; SLEDAI-2K (score \\>=8) and PGA (score \\>=1.0 on a 0 to 3 VAS)\n* Current receipt of \\>=1 of the following classes of standard therapies for the treatment of SLE at stable doses: oral corticosteroid (OCS), antimalarials, conventional immunosuppressants\n* Other inclusion criteria may apply\n* The Medical Monitor may be consulted if there are any questions related to eligibility criteria\n\nExclusion Criteria:\n\n* Pregnancy or breastfeeding\n* Presence of significant lupus-associated renal disease and/or renal impairment\n* Receipt of an excluded therapy, including any anti-CD20, anti-CD19 therapy less than 9 months prior to screening or during screening; or cyclophosphamide, tacrolimus, ciclosporin, or voclosporin during the 2 months prior to screening or during screening\n* Significant or uncontrolled medical disease which, in the investigator's opinion, would preclude patient participation\n* Known active infection of any kind or recent major episode of infection\n* Intolerance or contraindication to study therapies\n* Other exclusion criteria may apply",
