   "description": "The NPI was a measure of psychobehavioral disturbances, assessing the frequency and severity of disturbances in 12 domains, based on a caregiver interview. For each domain, there was also an assessment of caregiver burden. Frequency for each domain was rated on a 4-point scale (from 1=rarely to 4=very often) and severity on a 3-point scale (from 1=mild to 3=severe), with the score for each domain being the product of the frequency and severity scores, such that each domain was scored from 1 to 12. The NPI total score was the sum of the 12 domain scores, ranged from 0 (best) to 144 (worst). Higher score indicated worst psychobehavior.",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline (Day 1), Week 104"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Double-blind (DB) Treatment Period: Percentage of Participants With Progression From Clinical Dementia Rating-Global Score (CDR-GS) 0 to 0.5 or Higher, 0.5 to 1 or Higher, and 1 to 2 or Higher",
   "description": "Percentage of participants with progression from CDR-GS 0 to 0.5 or higher, 0.5 to 1 or higher, and 1 to 2 or higher was reported. The CDR was a subjectively rated outcome measure that serves as a global clinical staging instrument. Six domains were assessed and then synthesized to assign a Global CDR score. It included 3 cognitive and 3 functional domains: (1) memory, (2) orientation, (3) judgment and problem solving, (4) involvement in community affairs, (5) home and hobbies, and (6) personal care based on the CDR interview. CDR was scored 2 ways yielding a global CDR score (CDR-GS and CDR-SB). CDR was an ordinal parameter (scale 0 \\[least impairment\\] to 3 \\[severe impairment\\]). The CDR-GS was derived based on an algorithm and was scored of 0 (normal), 0.5 (very mild dementia), 1 (mild dementia), 2 (moderate dementia), and 3 (severe dementia). Score ranges from 0 to 3 and a high score on CDR-GS indicated a high disease severity.",
