  {
   "measure": "Period 1: Percentage of Participants Achieving a Static Physician's Global Assessment (sPGA) Score of 0 (Clear) or 1 (Almost Clear) With ≥2 Grade Improvement From Baseline at Week 16",
   "description": "Percentage of participants achieving a sPGA score of 0 (clear) or 1 (almost clear) with ≥2-grade improvement from baseline at Week 16. The investigator assessed psoriasis severity on a 5-point scale from 0 (clear) to 4 (severe):\n\n0 = Clear: No signs of psoriasis; post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation may occur\n\n1. = Almost clear: Normal to pink lesions; no thickening; no or minimal focal scaling\n2. = Mild: Pink to light red color; slight thickening; mainly fine scaling\n3. = Moderate: Dull bright red erythema; moderate thickening; moderate scaling\n4. = Severe: Bright to deep red; severe thickening with hard edges; coarse scaling covering most or all lesions",
   "timeFrame": "Week 16"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Period 1: Change From Baseline in PASI Score at Week 16",
   "description": "Least square mean was determined with mixed model repeated measures (MMRM) using an unstructured covariance matrix, including treatment, visit, treatment-by-visit interaction, baseline PASI score, prior biologic therapy (Yes/No), and geographic region as fixed effects.\n\nThe PASI measures psoriasis severity based on lesion characteristics and body surface area (BSA) affected. Erythema, thickness, and scaling are each scored from 0 (none) to 4 (very severe) across four regions: head, trunk, upper limbs, and lower limbs. Area involvement in each region is graded from 0 (none) to 6 (90-100%). The sum of severity scores for erythema, thickness, and scaling is multiplied by the degree of involvement for each anatomic region and a region-specific BSA weight (0.1, 0.3, 0.2, 0.4). Regional scores are summed to give a total PASI score ranging from 0 to 72, with higher values indicating greater severity.",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline, Week 16"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Period 1: Percent Change From Baseline in PASI Score at Week 16",
   "description": "Least square mean was determined with mixed model repeated measures (MMRM) using an unstructured covariance matrix, including treatment, visit, treatment-by-visit interaction, baseline PASI score, prior biologic therapy (Yes/No), and geographic region as fixed effects.\n\nThe PASI measures psoriasis severity based on lesion characteristics and body surface area (BSA) affected. Erythema, thickness, and scaling are each scored from 0 (none) to 4 (very severe) across four regions: head, trunk, upper limbs, and lower limbs. Area involvement in each region is graded from 0 (none) to 6 (90-100%). The sum of severity scores for erythema, thickness, and scaling is multiplied by the degree of involvement for each anatomic region and a region-specific BSA weight (0.1, 0.3, 0.2, 0.4). Regional scores are summed to give a total PASI score ranging from 0 to 72, with higher values indicating greater severity.",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline, Week 16"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Percentage of Participants Achieving ≥90% Reduction From Baseline in PASI-90 at Week 16",
   "description": "Percentage of participants achieving ≥90% reduction from baseline in PASI-90 at Week 16. The PASI measures psoriasis severity based on lesion characteristics and body surface area (BSA) affected. Erythema, thickness, and scaling are each scored from 0 (none) to 4 (very severe) across four regions: head, trunk, upper limbs, and lower limbs. Area involvement in each region is graded from 0 (none) to 6 (90-100%). The sum of severity scores for erythema, thickness, and scaling is multiplied by the degree of involvement for each anatomic region and a region-specific BSA weight (0.1, 0.3, 0.2, 0.4). Regional scores are summed to give a total PASI score ranging from 0 to 72, with higher values indicating greater severity.",
   "timeFrame": "Week 16"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Period 1: Percentage of Participants Achieving ≥100% Reduction From Baseline in PASI-100 at Week 16",
   "description": "Percentage of participants achieving ≥100% reduction from baseline in PASI-100 at Week 16. The PASI measures psoriasis severity based on lesion characteristics and body surface area (BSA) affected. Erythema, thickness, and scaling are each scored from 0 (none) to 4 (very severe) across four regions: head, trunk, upper limbs, and lower limbs. Area involvement in each region is graded from 0 (none) to 6 (90-100%). The sum of severity scores for erythema, thickness, and scaling is multiplied by the degree of involvement for each anatomic region and a region-specific BSA weight (0.1, 0.3, 0.2, 0.4). Regional scores are summed to give a total PASI score ranging from 0 to 72, with higher values indicating greater severity.",
   "timeFrame": "Week 16"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Period 1: Change From Baseline in Dermatology Life Quality Index (DLQI) at 16 Weeks",
   "description": "The DLQI is a simple 10-question validated questionnaire where, except for question 7, each question is rated from 0 (Not at all) to 3 (Very much). If question 7 is answered 'yes', this question is assigned a score of 3. If question 7 is answered 'No' or 'Not Relevant', this question is assigned a score 0, except if either 'a lot' or 'a little' is ticked. If so, this question is assigned a score of 2 or 1, respectively. Then, the DLQI total score is the sum of the 10 questions and ranges from 0 to 30, where higher scores indicate worst disease severity.",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline, Week 16"
  },
  {
   "measure": "Period 1: Change From Baseline in the Percentage of Body Surface Area (BSA) Affected at Week 16",
   "description": "Least square mean was determined with MMRM using an unstructured covariance matrix, including treatment, visit, treatment-by-visit interaction, prior biologic treatment for psoriasis (Yes/No), and baseline score as a covariate, with participant as a random effect baseline is the last non-missing pre-dose value.\n\nBSA is a measurement of the affected skin area. It was defined as the percentage of surface area of the body involved with the condition being assessed, (that is, plaque psoriasis). The overall BSA affected by psoriasis plaques is estimated based on the palm area of the participant hand (entire palmar surface or \"handprint\" including the fingers), which equates to approximately 1% of total BSA. Negative change from baseline indicates improvement.",
   "timeFrame": "Baseline, Week 16"
  }
 ],
 "eligibility": {
  "criteria": "Inclusion Criteria:\n\n* Male or female participant aged 18 years or older.\n* History of primarily plaque psoriasis for at least 6 months prior to the screening visit.\n* Has had stable psoriasis conditions for at least 3 months before screening.\n* Has moderate to severe plaque psoriasis as defined by a PASI score of ≥ 12 and an sPGA score of ≥ 3 at screening and Day 1.\n* Has plaque psoriasis covering ≥ 10% of the total BSA at screening and Day 1.\n* Deemed by the investigator to be eligible for phototherapy or systemic therapy.\n* Females of childbearing potential must agree to use a highly effective contraceptive method from at least 4 weeks prior to Day 1 until at least 4 weeks after the last dose of study product.\n\nExclusion Criteria:\n\n* Female who is breastfeeding, pregnant, lactating, or who is planning to become pregnant during the study.\n* Has evidence of erythrodermic, pustular, predominantly inverse or guttate psoriasis, or drug-induced psoriasis.\n* History of skin disease or presence of skin condition that, in the opinion of the investigator, would interfere with the study assessments.\n* Participant is known to have immune deficiency or is immunocompromised.\n* Has immune-mediated conditions commonly associated with psoriasis, such as psoriatic arthritis, active uveitis, inflammatory bowel disease, that currently require systemic treatment (including corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, or biologics).\n\nNote: Participants with immune-mediated conditions commonly associated with psoriasis that do not require systemic treatment may be included in the study.\n\n* Has used any topical medication that could affect psoriasis (including corticosteroids, retinoids, vitamin D analogues \\[such as calcipotriol\\], Janus kinase \\[JAK\\] inhibitors, or tar) within 2 weeks prior to Day 1.\n* Has used any systemic treatment that could affect psoriasis (including corticosteroids, oral retinoids, immunosuppressive medication, anakinra, methotrexate, cyclosporine, oral JAK inhibitors, or apremilast) within 4 weeks prior to Day 1.\n\nNote: Intranasal corticosteroids and inhaled corticosteroids are allowed. Eye and ear drops containing corticosteroids are also allowed.\n\n* Participant has received any ultraviolet B (UVB) phototherapy (including tanning beds) or excimer laser within 4 weeks prior to Day 1.\n* Participant has had psoralen and ultraviolet A (PUVA) treatment within 4 weeks prior to Day 1.\n* Participant has received treatment with an investigational or marketed TYK2 inhibitor.",
  "healthyVolunteers": false,
  "sex": "ALL",
  "minimumAge": "18 Years"
 },
 "siteCount": 63,
